00:01:00.325 --> 00:01:10.481
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:01:10.481 --> 00:01:14.530
&gt;&gt; Well it's a real honor to be here this
evening.

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:18.380
I'm not sure that it's possible to
moderate this crowd but

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:19.499
I'll give it my best shot.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:26.570
it's, it's of course an honor to be up
here with with the four of them.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:32.370
Anybody who's fortunate enough to be the
Dean of this institution stands on

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:37.450
the shoulders of all of ones predecessors
and to have four

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:43.150
such distinguished predecessors with us
this evening is a remarkable opportunity.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.000
And I, I feel so honored to be here with
them.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:52.950
The GSB today, I think is in, incredibly
good shape.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.940
We're sitting here in this fabulous
auditorium.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.760
The Cemex auditorium.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:01.370
In our still relatively new Knight
Management Center.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.430
We have embedded in it a new curriculum
that we put in place six years ago.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:13.900
We are the most selective business school
for MBA students.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:20.270
Taking still fewer than 400 a year out of
the roughly 6.5 to 7.5 thousand

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:26.220
who apply and we have really transformed
ourselves into a school that aspires

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:32.830
to admit a relatively small number of
incredibly high potential individuals.

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:37.240
And then, given the opportunity in a two
year in the case of the MBA or

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.300
a one year in, in the case of the Sloan
program.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.600
Highly immersive a transformational
experience that helps them

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:50.530
become change agents that can take on the
biggest challenges in the world.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:55.040
Because we all know that the world has,
has very significant changes.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:57.990
A whole variety of fields.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:02.240
And we aspire to educate those leaders,
who can go out and

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.880
really tackle these big challenges.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:10.940
And so the, the question i'd like to
really start with this evening is,

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.980
how did we really get here?

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:13.930
Right?
We, we look around,

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:19.540
we kind of know, know where we are and
this is part of an oral history project.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:24.720
And so I thought let's tell, let's tell
the story a little bit because we have

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.160
here on the stage the people who made that
history.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.100
And so I'm gonna start, R.J. with you.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:35.980
And what I'm gonna ask each of the deans
chronologically to do is,

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:40.640
tell us a little bit about what you found
when you came here.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.050
And what you tried to do.

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:46.970
And then, your own assessment of of what
you left when you,

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:48.560
you hand the, the reigns over.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.230
And we'll go around the group.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.130
And then that way we'll tell the story
over the decades.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.950
&gt;&gt; Thank you Garth, I'm glad to be here
this afternoon.

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.412
As a matter of fact I'm glad just to be
alive.

00:04:01.412 --> 00:04:02.321
[LAUGH]

00:04:02.321 --> 00:04:05.265
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Most people born in 1916 are dead.

00:04:05.265 --> 00:04:06.724
[LAUGH]

00:04:06.724 --> 00:04:10.750
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; When I agreed to take this job

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:17.270
we had full agreement that there would be
a public management program added.

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:19.880
And that all the students, or for

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.860
all the students in the school, the
curriculum would be expanded.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.950
The all would be expanded.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.400
When I arrived on, there, I understood.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:42.413
That there would be an increase in cost
because of this, when you hire professors

00:04:42.413 --> 00:04:47.205
like [UNKNOWN] and Harry Roland and George
Schultz, you know it's gonna cause

00:04:47.205 --> 00:04:52.460
something, but I was prepared to take on
the added cost of that, that undertaking.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:59.350
When I arrived on campus, I was shocked
because I learned that the new president,

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:04.360
Ken Penser, had set a rule that the
business school could not

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:08.070
spend any more than a fixed dollar amount,
believe it or not.

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:12.090
I had received a grant from the Ford
Foundation to

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:18.640
teach students from the school of
education, something about management.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:22.120
We spent the money on the course,
developed it,

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:27.110
got the check from Ford Foundation, which
went over to central administration.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:28.515
And Ken Kester pocketed it.

00:05:28.515 --> 00:05:29.090
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.120
&gt;&gt; We didn't get a dime of it.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:34.950
I insisted on getting a meeting with him.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:38.010
They're trying to get him to change the
rule, but he wouldn't talk to me for

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:40.025
the fist six months I was on campus.

00:05:40.025 --> 00:05:43.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I went over his head

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:49.130
to the chairman of the board of trustees
of Stanford at that time, Carma Fuller,

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:52.200
and told him that if I didn't get the rule
changed, I would resign.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:56.370
He was sympathetic to how I was being
mistreated.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.300
Ask please ask me to stay on and

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:02.880
be patient and that other people were
unhappy with Pister.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.740
I got the idea that Pister was gonna be
fired.

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.060
He was fired on August 1.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:14.540
Nick Lyman came in and in eight days I had
a five year

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:22.311
written contract that anybody but raised
above 500,000 could be kept.

00:06:22.311 --> 00:06:28.480
[UNKNOWN] was the dean of the business
school here at Stanford at that time.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:33.550
And he advised me that if we increase the
academic freedom of the school.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:37.160
We would get even more freedom, so we made
that a number one priority.

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.710
In those days, the deans of all the
business schools in the US voted.

00:06:42.710 --> 00:06:45.100
And in five years the voted us number one.

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:49.480
I think our inde, financial independence,
a,

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:54.260
additional freedom has served the business
school very well over these years, Garth.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:56.350
And that formula is still in place.

00:06:56.350 --> 00:06:59.700
You asked some other question but I wanna
talk too long but about-

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You know, what contributed to

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.870
good times we had but turn it over back to
you.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:04.370
I think-
&gt;&gt; Well-

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:05.680
&gt;&gt; That's how I got started here.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:06.570
It was a little rough but

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:07.662
it turned out okay.

00:07:07.662 --> 00:07:13.550
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
[APPLAUSE].

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:17.050
Well, thank you RJ and, and of course the,

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:22.360
the formula base that you established with
the University which has allowed us

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:26.410
to keep the money that our Alumni have
wanted to give for the specific purpose

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:30.380
of supporting the Business School has
served us in, incredibly well.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:34.140
Over the decades and we owe that to you.

00:07:34.140 --> 00:07:37.180
And so, from that onto you Bob.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:41.070
What did you find and, and what did you
really hope to do during your tenure?

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.900
&gt;&gt; Let me pick up int the 60s because I
was RJ's associate Dean and

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:50.409
that, he was really my mentor.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:58.900
it, it, it was I guess the other piece of
background you,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:03.810
you sort of have to cover is that, if you
looked at the industry, and

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:08.010
you said sort of how does business
education stack up.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:13.427
And, let's say that [COUGH],

00:08:13.427 --> 00:08:20.230
let's say the 50s and the 60s, early 60s,.

00:08:20.230 --> 00:08:24.760
It, it was, and keep in mind that the Ford
Foundation and

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:29.970
our own Jim Howl and Aaron Gordon,
economist up at the University of

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:35.410
California sort of did a landmark study on
the status of business education.

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:40.940
And so there was a blueprint that was sort
of beginning to emerge.

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:46.940
But, the, the idea was, you really need,
we

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:52.129
needed to upgrade academics of the program
and the, the kind of education that.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:59.980
That led to an MBA that led to any, it,
all, all of the professional program.

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:01.690
And, that didn't come just from us.

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:06.760
I mean that was, sort of the status of the
industry I guess as,

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.950
as viewed by well two economists.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.100
But, but it was a very good study.

00:09:13.100 --> 00:09:15.610
And there were others that sort of
corroborated that.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:22.330
So and, and one of the things that they
suggested would,

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:27.270
would improve would be to, to pull into
the business school

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:33.285
disciplines that in those days were a
little bit foreign to the business school.

00:09:33.285 --> 00:09:36.990
I.E. economics, behavioral science,
operations research.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:40.400
Today you'd say political economy but

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.750
whatever the version was of political
economy in those days, and that was,

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:51.610
and, and, and that sort of began I think
it's fair to say in the Arbuckle career.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:55.820
And all of us up here would, should be
very quick to tell you that,

00:09:55.820 --> 00:09:59.730
that you never start with something that's
blank.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:03.770
We all build on the shoulders of those who
came before us.

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:07.100
So, you know, it's kind of a tragedy
today, in some sense, I think,

00:10:07.100 --> 00:10:11.870
that you can't hear from Arbuckle and J.U.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:16.610
Jacks who brought you, brought us through
the Depression and World War II.

00:10:16.610 --> 00:10:17.860
That was no small feat.

00:10:19.980 --> 00:10:24.880
And he was dean for 25 years so it, you
know, we, we, we started with

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:30.880
a school that had high quality even in
those days and the,

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:33.820
the idea was, how do you improve it?

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:34.510
How do you improve it?

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.940
How do you get kind of, move it along to
where it is today?

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:47.370
To infuse the disciplines of the business
school, then you had to help them

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:52.960
try to figure out how do you bring your
discipline to bear on something,

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.680
on the profession programs which was to
say managerial leadership.

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:04.770
And it wasn't obvious, but there were some
guidelines that you can begin with.

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:08.870
Well I, I think it in, the, the,

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:15.900
the school as I knew it and sort of in, in
RJ's era is when it kind of begin.

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:21.860
And this I don't know maybe sounds a
little corny today, but the, the, the,

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:30.130
somehow there emerged a name that said, we
called it balanced excellence.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:34.889
What it really meant was.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:41.760
We wanna do, a, as a single faculty, not,
not have specialists so that some did one

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:47.870
thing and some did another, but as a
faculty we wanna do outstanding research.

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:53.670
But we also wanna do an outstanding job of
teaching, formulating, and

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:55.380
teaching the professional programs.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.110
And to do it as a single faculty.

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:04.350
That's like saying you wanna to do the R&amp;D
and

00:12:04.350 --> 00:12:08.250
the production and the sales all in the
same group.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:10.000
That is not an easy model.

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:18.320
And but, but we felt that was the best way
to do things like bring

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:25.050
the disciplines to bear on managerial
education, leadership and, and management.

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:31.310
We also felt that, that any of the
professional programs

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:35.850
needed to be rooted in, in strong academic
research.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:43.170
And you have to keep in mind, research is
sort of the only activity that people and

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:47.680
that faculty engage in, it's that, that
builds their intellectual capital.

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:51.560
Practically everything else we do, you,
you, you're living off your capital.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:59.990
Well we started that model, and as, and,
and we made it work.

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:07.660
But of all the things that are good in
almost any academic university,

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:10.160
when you say, how does it start?

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:13.760
It's the faculty and the students who make
it happen.

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:20.680
You know, the, the, the, the deans can try
to help formulate the direction.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:28.710
RJ was great like Ernie was at providing
resources, so that one of the things that

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:36.500
struck me when I came here was, I never
heard the words, I can't or we can't.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:41.490
We heard the words like well let's see
what we have to do to do it.

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:45.650
And so good ideas were implemented.

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:51.260
But when you say where do the good ideas
come from and how did they implement it,

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:55.430
you've got to have a first class faculty
very deep in their disciplines.

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:59.960
Who wanted to take on managerial problems.

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:01.910
They wanted to lead.

00:14:01.910 --> 00:14:06.210
That's something your faculty has was
noted for

00:14:06.210 --> 00:14:11.540
at the very beginning is leading the
changes that are going to occur.

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:15.480
But there's only so many hours in a day.

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:21.620
Research and teaching are very different
activities in some sense and it,

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:25.530
it's like I'm sure the faculty felt, we're
living in two worlds.

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:28.420
we, we need about a 28 or

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:33.090
a 30-hour a day to get all this done, but
it's the same on the students.

00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:37.390
You have to have first class students who
wanna learn in that kind of

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:37.950
an environment.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:45.610
And that's always been the characteristic,
so the idea was, strong academic programs.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:50.290
Professional programs resting on
outstanding research,

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:51.730
outstanding scholarship.

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:55.889
And the other thing was, if you could link
those two together.

00:14:55.889 --> 00:14:58.836
Th, the courses would keep up.

00:14:58.836 --> 00:15:03.353
They would even lead the changing who were
taking place in the world.

00:15:03.353 --> 00:15:05.698
And you can see this today.

00:15:05.698 --> 00:15:10.450
I, I, I still read, with this one last
comment.

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:15.190
I still read the annual report very
carefully.

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:19.170
Well if you look at the annual report, for
example for 2012,

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:26.130
you find something like about 60
publications.

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:31.150
But you also find 25 new courses and I bet
that if

00:15:31.150 --> 00:15:36.650
you looked at the change from one year to
the next in the elective structure.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:42.390
You'd probably find 20, 25% new material
in those courses as well.

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:44.080
And so, you say where does that come from?

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:47.690
Well it comes from the academic, from the
research base.

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:52.630
And the faculty and the students are the
ones that make that happen, and

00:15:52.630 --> 00:15:56.720
you have to have that that interchange.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:00.200
And of course, you all have mentioned the
PhD program there is no

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:05.440
question that the PhD program in this
school is an outstanding program.

00:16:05.440 --> 00:16:08.370
And it's got to rest on a research base.

00:16:08.370 --> 00:16:10.440
There's got to be links to a research
base.

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:16.780
But, you got the faculty and students to
thank for making this happen.

00:16:16.780 --> 00:16:19.590
I guess it's very gratifying, I'm sure it
is to RJ,

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:22.280
it is to me, that they're still doing it.

00:16:22.280 --> 00:16:23.330
&gt;&gt; Mm.

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:25.280
&gt;&gt; That they're still doing it.

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:29.150
And all you have to do is look at the
program today,

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:34.740
and if anything you'd probably have more
political economy.

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:38.085
You got more of the disciplines reflecting
themselves.

00:16:38.085 --> 00:16:40.100
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; I've spoken too long.

00:16:40.100 --> 00:16:41.140
&gt;&gt; No.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:16:41.140 --> 00:16:44.620
&gt;&gt; No and I'm, I'm gonna, I'm gonna ask
you a follow on before I, I turn to Mike.

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:47.100
Because, you know, one of the things that
fascinates me,

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.250
I mean it is, it is a challenge even
today.

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:54.610
To, to hire and maintain the quality of
faculty that we do.

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:58.120
That's been an enduring challenge I think
for all of us.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:03.584
And I, I wonder what it was like to try to
convince a disciplined based set

00:17:03.584 --> 00:17:09.065
of scholars to come to what was really a
new adventure in those days.

00:17:09.065 --> 00:17:11.700
Cuz there was not that was not an
established model.

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:14.901
&gt;&gt; How, how'd we the fa-

00:17:14.901 --> 00:17:16.450
&gt;&gt; How to get the faculty to-

00:17:16.450 --> 00:17:18.900
&gt;&gt; Well, I, I think we did two things.

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:26.405
One was, we were able to convince some
senior scholars, who came here.

00:17:26.405 --> 00:17:32.000
That were representative of, of
disciplines that were new to the business.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:35.370
I came here as a faculty member in 1960,
61, I guess.

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:38.085
And I, I was an old accountant.

00:17:38.085 --> 00:17:40.700
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But who were the others?

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:47.830
Well Ezra Solomon, Allen Mann, Al Hastorf
the,

00:17:47.830 --> 00:17:53.900
who came as the chairman of the psychology
department at Dartmouth, you know?

00:17:55.114 --> 00:18:00.583
Fred Fong was a computer scientist from
Carnegie Mellon.

00:18:00.583 --> 00:18:04.120
I, I was not the only, you know he said
well,

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:07.620
could you recognize somebody' who's
familiar with a business school?

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:10.670
I was probably the only one who group up
in the business school.

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:11.990
And that, that continued.

00:18:11.990 --> 00:18:16.310
They got you know, there were, and the
people that came here then,

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:20.910
and again if you hadn't had that faculty
does the recruiting.

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:25.830
So the faculty had to help convince those
that,

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:28.989
that did come that were sorta at that
senior level that.

00:18:30.390 --> 00:18:31.290
A, we needed them.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:32.840
B, they were relevant.

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:35.800
C, they had to, the, the, the, they, they,

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:42.170
they had to learn how their discipline
could help to improve management.

00:18:42.170 --> 00:18:47.080
But then the next phase was now is really
the scary one.

00:18:48.170 --> 00:18:54.159
Was you go out and you try to find young,
beginning,

00:18:54.159 --> 00:18:59.490
discipline based, research based young
people.

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:03.010
And you bring them in and you help them
mature.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:03.840
You help them grown.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:06.980
You help them develop.

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:09.960
And there's any number of those people
around today that

00:19:11.520 --> 00:19:15.750
I've been here for a number of years.

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:18.230
And that's scary.

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:22.970
Because one thing the faculty had to do
and boy I'll and I,

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:26.310
I, I got to tell you this but I didn't
invent it.

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:28.700
I just tried to push it along.

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:33.350
If you had a group that were here, that
did the, the recruiting, and

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:36.240
they were trying to find people who were
better than they were.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:40.800
Now you gotta stop and think for a moment,
what does that mean?

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:44.305
It means that maybe I ought to become a
Dean.

00:19:44.305 --> 00:19:46.844
[LAUGH] Because I'm not gonna survive in
this atmosphere

00:19:46.844 --> 00:19:47.720
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:53.250
&gt;&gt; No I, you know, you, that, that sounds
funny but boy, it, it's real.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:57.850
I mean, and but, but the faculty had to do
that.

00:19:57.850 --> 00:19:59.400
And they were willing and, and

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:04.400
what it meant was if you appoint people
who are smarter than you are.

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:10.680
It probably also means they come here with
a different set of talents.

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:12.230
They're different then you are.

00:20:13.518 --> 00:20:15.030
And you sorta have to step back and

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:18.530
say, how many organizations do you know
that are willing to do that.

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:21.050
That still goes on today I'm sure.

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:23.720
&gt;&gt; Well I, I think it's, I think it's a
remarkable thing that.

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:24.610
&gt;&gt; Pardon me?
&gt;&gt; I think it's

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:25.895
a remarkable thing that you did.

00:20:25.895 --> 00:20:26.865
[CROSSTALK].

00:20:26.865 --> 00:20:33.360
&gt;&gt; Well, but, but, but the, it, it was
done at that, at that time at the school.

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:35.671
You know, I, I don't, I don't know how
widely appreciated it is.

00:20:35.671 --> 00:20:39.051
But really, at that time, the Stanford
Graduate School of

00:20:39.051 --> 00:20:42.710
Business defined the model for the modern
business school.

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:44.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And that, that was the model, and

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:48.320
it is the model that has endured to, to
today.

00:20:48.320 --> 00:20:51.820
And has really created the model for for
the industry.

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:55.451
&gt;&gt; I, I think, I, so we, we have
leadership position in the industries.

00:20:55.451 --> 00:20:56.811
RJ said it.

00:20:56.811 --> 00:21:01.550
Keep in mind that, what, in 1975?

00:21:01.550 --> 00:21:06.310
This school rep or celebrated its 50th
anniversary.

00:21:07.380 --> 00:21:10.350
And by the time the 50th anniversary, this
is only 10,

00:21:10.350 --> 00:21:14.490
12 years after sorta early post-war.

00:21:14.490 --> 00:21:18.360
Sorta started the, kinda started during
his era.

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:19.270
It was like 15 years.

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:23.740
The industry was saying you're the best
school in the world.

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:29.190
Now I, I don't think anybody, you know,
got too big headed about that.

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:31.770
But, but it was very very gratifying to

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:36.308
say the outside industry recognizes the
leadership of this school.

00:21:36.308 --> 00:21:37.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:37.190 --> 00:21:39.384
Incredible.

00:21:39.384 --> 00:21:41.340
So Mike, you and I came together.

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:41.920
&gt;&gt; We did.
&gt;&gt; We,

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:45.209
you know, we came back, both of us in
different forms.

00:21:46.570 --> 00:21:49.880
and, so, tell us a little bit about you
know, the decade of the, of the 90s.

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:55.153
&gt;&gt; Well, you've already heard the
foundation that we were standing on at

00:21:55.153 --> 00:21:55.783
that point.

00:21:55.783 --> 00:22:02.220
Well established and, and at one,
basically, competitively, in the industry.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:06.190
I think the people who built it, including
Bob and RJ.

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:12.510
And Ernie and Jim Howell, and, and Jim Van
Horn and a whole group of people.

00:22:13.870 --> 00:22:17.890
Showed a lot of imagination and also
courage.

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:20.940
I mean, it, you know, this is a model that
sounds great now.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:24.230
But it wasn't obvious at the time it would
work.

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:27.640
And I have to say, I wouldn't be here.

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:29.740
I mean, I was an academic.

00:22:29.740 --> 00:22:32.700
If that hadn't been the model, I wouldn't
have been back at Stanford.

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:35.660
In this wonderful job.

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:43.160
Let me however say, you know, like any
great model, you know,

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.560
there's stresses and strains that come.

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:51.760
And at the end of Bob's time, and
certainly moving into the 90s, there was,

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:54.760
there started to be questioning of this
model.

00:22:56.420 --> 00:22:59.640
The symbol of it, of course, was the
Business Week survey.

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:04.922
The, you know, did an awful lot of good
things for their sales of magazines, but

00:23:04.922 --> 00:23:09.410
put, kinda put us into a bit of a tailspin
and slightly on the defensive.

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:13.715
and, and I think what they were saying is,

00:23:13.715 --> 00:23:18.850
[INAUDIBLE]
something in the balance.

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:19.980
You know, was out of whack.

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:21.890
Now part of it was nuts.

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:28.640
I, when I, when I got here well known
alumus of the school came in to see me.

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:34.060
And said, you know, the graduates of this
school aren't aggressive enough.

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:36.680
You know, they don't go kill people, you
know.

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:40.060
We hire Harvard type, and I said, well.

00:23:40.060 --> 00:23:41.130
But I kinda like them.

00:23:41.130 --> 00:23:43.628
And he said well they just organize the
social functions.

00:23:43.628 --> 00:23:47.158
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:23:47.158 --> 00:23:51.342
I actually think that part of the culture
of the school was built along with this.

00:23:51.342 --> 00:23:55.540
This sorta cohesiveness has turned out to
be another great strength.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:59.350
A more worrying set of phone calls came in
that sounded more or less.

00:23:59.350 --> 00:24:03.770
Remember this is when Japan was, you know,
fairly far along, and

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:07.510
we thought eating our lunch seems like
ancient history now.

00:24:07.510 --> 00:24:11.490
I got phone calls saying, you know, the
whole MBA program ought to be scrapped and

00:24:11.490 --> 00:24:13.950
replaced with total quality management
programs.

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:17.850
And I thought, thank you very much, put
down the phone.

00:24:17.850 --> 00:24:23.530
And, but we did, in some sense, feel we
had to respond to this.

00:24:23.530 --> 00:24:28.055
And one of the things that, eh, that I
wanted to add, in this kinda history, is,

00:24:28.055 --> 00:24:29.578
one of the things we tried.

00:24:29.578 --> 00:24:38.030
Garth was involved in this Irv Grosset,
Chuck Holloway, many, many people.

00:24:38.030 --> 00:24:44.210
We evolved a model that, that Bob Joss
carried on, that involves centers.

00:24:44.210 --> 00:24:47.170
It involved expanding the curriculum.

00:24:47.170 --> 00:24:52.670
It involved bringing very talented people
from the financial side,

00:24:52.670 --> 00:24:57.260
from business, practitioners if you like,
to come and teach together.

00:24:57.260 --> 00:24:58.810
You know, with our own faculty.

00:24:58.810 --> 00:24:59.780
Garth has done this.

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:01.669
Robert Burgelman did it with Andy Grove.

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:05.970
And so we, we adapted the model.

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:09.550
I think in a way that was consistent with
the underlying ideals.

00:25:09.550 --> 00:25:10.280
&gt;&gt; Yeah it is.
&gt;&gt; I mean,

00:25:10.280 --> 00:25:13.700
I always said teaching Executive
Education,

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:16.950
teaching with Andy Grove if you ever got
the chance.

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:23.380
You know, or more recently with with Pete
Wendell,.

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:24.390
And, they, they,

00:25:24.390 --> 00:25:28.720
you discover things that could get
transformed into your research agenda.

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:31.460
It was intellectually enriching in the
long run.

00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:36.470
And so I think that has turned out over a
period of a couple of decades to

00:25:36.470 --> 00:25:38.660
be a really important addition.

00:25:40.320 --> 00:25:45.070
To the way the school operates, building,
building on that model and the foundation.

00:25:45.070 --> 00:25:46.610
The, the other thing I wanted to say.

00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:50.903
I was sitting, sitting at home the other
night in Milan ,.

00:25:50.903 --> 00:25:53.051
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:25:53.051 --> 00:25:54.965
Italy is a bit of a wreck right now.

00:25:54.965 --> 00:25:57.595
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think you should all take cover.

00:25:57.595 --> 00:25:58.897
[LAUGH].

00:25:58.897 --> 00:26:05.220
Thinking about what RJ was doing with the
public management program,

00:26:05.220 --> 00:26:06.610
which is the first thing he mentioned.

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:11.440
You know, when you look around the world,
it's astonishing how much is

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:16.090
dependent on political and policy
reactions through their actions.

00:26:16.090 --> 00:26:17.440
And I remember thinking.

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:22.640
Could I possibly have had the foresight to
see that politics and

00:26:22.640 --> 00:26:27.600
political economy were gonna become an
increasingly important part of our life.

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:30.260
And I, I concluded the answer was no.

00:26:30.260 --> 00:26:34.310
That R.J. was probably unique in that
respect.

00:26:34.310 --> 00:26:35.390
But it really is true.

00:26:36.500 --> 00:26:38.080
The other thing we started and,

00:26:38.080 --> 00:26:42.702
and then it really gained momentum under,
under Bob Joss.

00:26:42.702 --> 00:26:46.054
Is you know, we're living in a world in
which

00:26:46.054 --> 00:26:51.983
the developing countries that account for
85% of the world's population.

00:26:51.983 --> 00:26:54.050
Are now 50% of the global economy.

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:59.440
And business people, and we on our own
faculty, were saying, you know,

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:01.540
we've got to react to this.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:05.960
And so, we piled on an additional
challenge, which is,

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:10.382
somehow we have to sort of build into our
search agenda and the curriculum.

00:27:10.382 --> 00:27:15.790
A way to keep track, of this, rapidly
evolving world that's having a larger and

00:27:15.790 --> 00:27:17.970
larger impact on us.

00:27:17.970 --> 00:27:23.420
Remember in the 1960s, what went on in the
developing world,

00:27:23.420 --> 00:27:28.280
you know, occasionally caused a fit on the
balance sheet of, you know, Citibank.

00:27:28.280 --> 00:27:30.650
But for the most part, was largely
irrelevant.

00:27:30.650 --> 00:27:36.150
And we were a big, dominant economy, 40%
of the global economy.

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:39.360
And, you know, we operated in that world.

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:42.060
That, that's, that's a world now.

00:27:42.060 --> 00:27:44.220
That we're continuing to have to adapt to.

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:49.930
And so, you overlay on it the need to have
a research agenda with a discipline base.

00:27:49.930 --> 00:27:53.990
And work together to build an, an, a, a,
a, you know, a,

00:27:53.990 --> 00:27:57.202
a, imaginative, creative curriculum.

00:27:57.202 --> 00:28:00.202
For a bunch of people who want to be sorta
managers, and

00:28:00.202 --> 00:28:04.040
leaders, and NGO's in business and
finance, and so on.

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:06.800
And then, somehow you've got to find a
way.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:09.700
And I know Garth still wrestles with this.

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:14.280
To sorta keep your finger on the pulse of
of

00:28:14.280 --> 00:28:18.950
an enormously broad array of sorta
economic activity.

00:28:18.950 --> 00:28:24.490
The, the last thing I'll say is it's very
important to pick the time you arrive.

00:28:24.490 --> 00:28:28.730
And, and the time you depart, so I learned
my lesson.

00:28:28.730 --> 00:28:29.590
I showed up in 1990.

00:28:29.590 --> 00:28:34.910
A recession that they concocted in the
East Coast of the United States,

00:28:36.070 --> 00:28:41.606
was rolling west with great speed,
unbeknownst to many people out here.

00:28:41.606 --> 00:28:45.174
And it hit with a storm and then the
university got

00:28:45.174 --> 00:28:50.344
into a fairly large amount of trouble with
the Federal Government.

00:28:50.344 --> 00:28:54.390
Over the sponsored research sorta overhead
cost recovery.

00:28:54.390 --> 00:28:56.000
So, some of you will remember.

00:28:57.040 --> 00:29:00.484
And so that was fu, and there'd been an
earthquake in 1989.

00:29:00.484 --> 00:29:02.053
So Paul Johnson and, and

00:29:02.053 --> 00:29:06.360
many others were trying to keep the old
school from falling down.

00:29:06.360 --> 00:29:08.145
And I thought well this is a great way to
start.

00:29:08.145 --> 00:29:10.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:29:10.310 --> 00:29:13.730
&gt;&gt; And somewhere along the way Don Kennedy
stepped aside and so on.

00:29:13.730 --> 00:29:19.130
Well anyway, it got a lot better and by
the time I stepped down we had

00:29:19.130 --> 00:29:23.980
the internet running at high speed, it was
marvelous we were awash in cash.

00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:28.340
And I thought to myself, this isn't gonna
last.

00:29:28.340 --> 00:29:29.868
And I better get out of the way.

00:29:29.868 --> 00:29:31.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So that.

00:29:31.460 --> 00:29:33.210
&gt;&gt; Funny, that's what I thought.

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:35.980
&gt;&gt; So that Bob, Bob Joss could take over
when the bubble burst.

00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:38.400
I didn't know it was gonna be Bob, but.

00:29:38.400 --> 00:29:43.360
And so I made up this excuse that I'd been
a Dean for 15 years, including 9 here.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:49.490
And that my successor ought to preside
over the 75th anniversary of the school.

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:51.273
I declared complete victory.

00:29:51.273 --> 00:29:54.076
And turned it over to a world in

00:29:54.076 --> 00:29:59.701
which we had a big downturn economically
followed by 9/11.

00:29:59.701 --> 00:30:00.712
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:30:00.712 --> 00:30:01.779
&gt;&gt; And over to you, Bob.

00:30:01.779 --> 00:30:04.159
&gt;&gt; Well before, before we, we, we, we go
there, Mike.

00:30:04.159 --> 00:30:04.996
I'm.

00:30:04.996 --> 00:30:09.094
Reminded by seeing Carol Dressler in in
the audience.

00:30:09.094 --> 00:30:09.697
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:30:09.697 --> 00:30:12.540
&gt;&gt; That we have we have spoken about the
MBA program.

00:30:12.540 --> 00:30:18.220
We've, we've referenced the Sloane program
and and Bob spoke about the PhD program.

00:30:18.220 --> 00:30:20.080
But we've not said anything about
Executive Education.

00:30:20.080 --> 00:30:20.630
&gt;&gt; True.

00:30:20.630 --> 00:30:23.620
&gt;&gt; Which has been a very important part of
the school, and

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:25.870
an increasingly part of the school.

00:30:25.870 --> 00:30:31.800
And I remember, you and, and Carolyn, and
Jeff Pfeffer and some others.

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:36.800
Wrestling with, how to approach that,
within the model that's been described.

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:39.300
I if you could say just a couple of words
about that.

00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:46.070
&gt;&gt; Well and again, I don't mean this to
be, to chip away at the, at the model.

00:30:46.070 --> 00:30:50.680
But Jim Howell in my first or second year
as Dean, came in and said Mike.

00:30:50.680 --> 00:30:55.860
We're carrying Executive Education on the,
on the shoulder of, of about five or

00:30:55.860 --> 00:30:58.370
six older faculty.

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:03.800
So the theory that we have a school that's
engaging with experienced executives

00:31:05.290 --> 00:31:08.250
broadly as the faculty, is not, in fact,
the case.

00:31:08.250 --> 00:31:10.980
And, and he said this is very vulnerable
and

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:15.950
he actually led a faculty group to sorta
study this question.

00:31:15.950 --> 00:31:19.120
And we, then experimented with ways to

00:31:19.120 --> 00:31:22.500
bring younger people in a non threatening
way.

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:27.100
I mean the MBA is already relatively scary
for

00:31:27.100 --> 00:31:30.590
a newly admitted PhD in, pick your
discipline.

00:31:32.210 --> 00:31:34.800
But an executive program is truly scary
and

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:37.710
yet, and, and, and the what, the way we
approached that.

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:40.425
Garth, you and I were together doing this.

00:31:40.425 --> 00:31:44.320
we, we had to prepare both sides.

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:47.480
We had to help the junior faculty go in
there and we had to give them

00:31:47.480 --> 00:31:51.820
an assignment where they were operating
with their comparative advantage.

00:31:51.820 --> 00:31:54.720
And we had to prepare the listeners, you
know,

00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:57.000
the participants in the program and say
look.

00:31:57.000 --> 00:31:59.917
The majority of what you're gonna get is
the kinda thing you expected when

00:31:59.917 --> 00:32:00.538
you came here.

00:32:00.538 --> 00:32:04.427
But you're gonna be exposed to some young
people who aren't gonna be able to

00:32:04.427 --> 00:32:07.470
speak your language in toto as an
experienced executive.

00:32:07.470 --> 00:32:11.330
But you're gonna hear some pretty
amazingly interesting ideas from these

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:12.370
young people.

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:15.880
And that, that after some experimentation
I think, you know,

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:17.377
has produced a situation.

00:32:17.377 --> 00:32:23.350
Which the executive programs have broader
faculty participation.

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:26.200
We probably ought to give Carol a second
opinion on this.

00:32:26.200 --> 00:32:27.800
&gt;&gt; Well, and they're really thriving now,

00:32:27.800 --> 00:32:30.310
an, an, they, they've got their, a big
boost.

00:32:30.310 --> 00:32:33.000
And so, Bob?

00:32:33.000 --> 00:32:33.850
Now over, over to you.

00:32:35.570 --> 00:32:37.761
&gt;&gt; Well that's a great set up by Mike.

00:32:37.761 --> 00:32:43.216
1999, if you can remember, was NASDAQ
5000.

00:32:43.216 --> 00:32:48.110
And CNBC on the lawn interviewing our
students out here.

00:32:48.110 --> 00:32:49.725
It was nirvana.

00:32:49.725 --> 00:32:50.795
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:32:52.690 --> 00:32:55.450
&gt;&gt; And more seriously, it's, it was a
great school.

00:32:55.450 --> 00:33:00.650
I mean, we had this foundation starting
with Ernie to RJ to Bob to Mike.

00:33:00.650 --> 00:33:06.180
Great students, great faculty, students
who were in both MBA,

00:33:06.180 --> 00:33:08.600
PhD, executive students.

00:33:10.310 --> 00:33:12.420
An international reputation.

00:33:12.420 --> 00:33:14.370
You know, so.

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:15.490
What do you do for an encore?

00:33:17.180 --> 00:33:22.510
But I think like you know, all great
institutions the real secret is,

00:33:22.510 --> 00:33:24.910
can you keep moving it forward?

00:33:24.910 --> 00:33:26.140
Can you keep challenging it?

00:33:26.140 --> 00:33:28.620
John Gardner used to say, you gotta renew
yourself.

00:33:28.620 --> 00:33:34.091
And I always figure the role of a leader
is to see if you can change it for

00:33:34.091 --> 00:33:39.669
the better, and get people excited about
changing it for the better.

00:33:39.669 --> 00:33:41.730
And the secret I think for

00:33:41.730 --> 00:33:46.609
us, at my time, was engagement, you know,
to sit down with faulty.

00:33:48.000 --> 00:33:49.910
Who always had ides about how it could be
better,

00:33:49.910 --> 00:33:55.770
staff, students, alumni, recruiters, the
university.

00:33:57.090 --> 00:34:01.424
And I found as I got more engagement
going, a lot of people had ideas about how

00:34:01.424 --> 00:34:04.901
it could be better and perhaps how it
could be more balanced.

00:34:04.901 --> 00:34:07.530
You know you're always trying to keep
things in balance.

00:34:07.530 --> 00:34:12.510
It might be great for students but faculty
morale was down, or it maybe great for

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:14.690
faculty, but staff morale is down.

00:34:14.690 --> 00:34:19.820
So you're, you're trying to keep everybody
in a plane where they

00:34:19.820 --> 00:34:23.730
think wow I'm part of a great school, and
I'm part of helping it to be great.

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:28.820
So one thing led to another and I think
that process of engagement,

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:33.000
of talking to a lot of people about simply
answering that question.

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:35.040
You know how can we make a great school
even better?

00:34:36.262 --> 00:34:38.916
We began to hit on three big themes.

00:34:38.916 --> 00:34:40.552
You know that we call the three C's,
[COUGH].

00:34:40.552 --> 00:34:44.110
So I can remember them every time I went
out to talk.

00:34:44.110 --> 00:34:49.010
The first C was curriculum, that we
thought we needed a new curriculum.

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:50.200
To really engage students,

00:34:50.200 --> 00:34:55.610
one that was more global, one that was
more tailored to individual differences.

00:34:55.610 --> 00:34:58.300
One that was more about leadership and
communication.

00:34:58.300 --> 00:35:02.028
And a faculty group headed by our next
Dean, Garth,

00:35:02.028 --> 00:35:08.324
really put forward an astonishing and, and
exciting and challenging new curriculum.

00:35:08.324 --> 00:35:13.100
That we thought would bring the culture
much farther along than it was.

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:15.140
So that was one thing.

00:35:15.140 --> 00:35:17.910
Then I thought, a new collaboration with
Stanford.

00:35:17.910 --> 00:35:21.430
We were beginning to enter an era where
Stanford was doing so

00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:25.520
many exciting things, tackling big
challenges, big problems.

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:29.949
Human heath, the environment international
peace and development.

00:35:31.800 --> 00:35:36.280
and, and so we we just thought there were
ways in which we as a school could be

00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:38.510
more engaged with the university and vice
versa.

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:44.580
Our students with other graduate students,
our faculty with, graduate students.

00:35:44.580 --> 00:35:47.407
Who are gonna become future faculty
members and or

00:35:47.407 --> 00:35:49.636
participants in other institutions.

00:35:49.636 --> 00:35:54.789
So that collaboration was a secondcy and
the combination of that led me

00:35:54.789 --> 00:35:58.560
to something I never expected to even
worry about.

00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:00.910
Which was a new campus, because we,

00:36:00.910 --> 00:36:04.360
when I came I inherited really some great
facilities.

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:07.800
Mike had been successful in getting the
Schwab Center across the street built.

00:36:07.800 --> 00:36:11.759
That was a transforming investment in the
school.

00:36:11.759 --> 00:36:14.781
He had the new night building that you was
you know,

00:36:14.781 --> 00:36:17.040
hooked onto the little field center.

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:20.035
And so, we, we thought no, we're okay for
facilities.

00:36:20.035 --> 00:36:23.823
But, increasingly we just thought for that
curriculum and for

00:36:23.823 --> 00:36:27.557
that collaboration the, the kinda
infrastructure we need.

00:36:27.557 --> 00:36:32.740
To enable all of this to really come to
life and really sing was a new campus.

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:38.840
So, that curriculum collaboration campus
course did lead to

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:41.515
a fourth C I didn't talk much about, which
is cash.

00:36:41.515 --> 00:36:44.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:48.810
&gt;&gt; Again though, you stand on the
shoulders of those who've gone before.

00:36:48.810 --> 00:36:54.700
I mean, Mike had done a fabulous job of
cultivating some very supportive alums.

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:59.412
People like Phil Knight, Bob Bass like
Chuck Schwab.

00:36:59.412 --> 00:37:02.655
And who continued to be excited about the
school and

00:37:02.655 --> 00:37:06.310
we could add to that a whole team of
people.

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:09.890
Who had found Stanford to be
transformative in their lives.

00:37:09.890 --> 00:37:11.580
And they wanted to make that possible for

00:37:11.580 --> 00:37:15.680
other and, you know its fantastic what we
have today.

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:16.950
But it gets better.

00:37:16.950 --> 00:37:20.400
You know, and I think as long as we
continue to try to change it

00:37:20.400 --> 00:37:21.830
for the better.

00:37:21.830 --> 00:37:24.670
Then that's, that's what a dean's all
about.

00:37:24.670 --> 00:37:29.714
So you try to pass that baton to the last
person.

00:37:29.714 --> 00:37:31.080
RJ followed Ernie,

00:37:31.080 --> 00:37:35.040
Ernie said you know ten years, you gotta
repot, that's enough time.

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:38.110
RJ said ten years, that's enough time.

00:37:38.110 --> 00:37:41.790
Bob said that, Mike said nine, I don't
know, he trumped up this nine year

00:37:41.790 --> 00:37:46.450
thing that I said ten years that's enough
time and over to you.

00:37:46.450 --> 00:37:48.500
&gt;&gt; Well thank you, thank you for that.

00:37:48.500 --> 00:37:52.370
So Mike made the comment that that it's
all about timing.

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:55.640
And and I'm the one up here that got that
right.

00:37:56.800 --> 00:37:59.960
So so you want to become Dean.

00:37:59.960 --> 00:38:06.050
When you're putting the finish touches on
a brand new eight building,

00:38:06.050 --> 00:38:07.950
Knight Management Center.

00:38:07.950 --> 00:38:11.240
When the curriculum is in place and and
really, how many,

00:38:11.240 --> 00:38:16.630
when we're you know, more than halfway
through an incredible campaign.

00:38:16.630 --> 00:38:18.910
That had as a goal, 500 million.

00:38:18.910 --> 00:38:24.200
Which, I think at the time we'd launched
it seemed out of reach and and and

00:38:24.200 --> 00:38:27.050
ended up way surpassing that thanks to.

00:38:27.050 --> 00:38:30.820
The, the incredible generosity of our
alumni.

00:38:30.820 --> 00:38:32.200
So I want to say just a couple of things
and

00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:36.500
then I'm gonna throw a few more questions
to this group.

00:38:36.500 --> 00:38:44.901
One is that as I listen to their
recounting what they did over the decades.

00:38:44.901 --> 00:38:49.477
One of the things that really strikes me
is that all of

00:38:49.477 --> 00:38:54.376
these elements that they described are
part of our DNA.

00:38:54.376 --> 00:38:59.290
They are very much who we still are today.

00:38:59.290 --> 00:39:03.430
So we rely on the formula model that you
built.

00:39:03.430 --> 00:39:08.090
We still have in place the faculty hiring
and

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:11.550
development model that was was putting
place several decades ago.

00:39:11.550 --> 00:39:15.200
Which is the core, the core strength of
the school.

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:18.972
Balanced excellence, we still talk about
in practice.

00:39:18.972 --> 00:39:22.289
You know, the integration of the [UNKNOWN]
line faculty with

00:39:22.289 --> 00:39:26.720
great practitioners often at the same time
in the same classroom.

00:39:26.720 --> 00:39:29.580
Right?
Which you know really, you gave the,

00:39:29.580 --> 00:39:33.360
the, the impetus to, the, the group, the
collaboration with the university.

00:39:33.360 --> 00:39:37.380
The fact that we are part of an incredible
university,

00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:39.120
walking distance to every school.

00:39:39.120 --> 00:39:43.329
This is something that virtually no other
business school has all of

00:39:43.329 --> 00:39:46.579
these elements which you, you know defined
your eras.

00:39:46.579 --> 00:39:51.213
Have become part of our DNA and have
continued and and

00:39:51.213 --> 00:39:56.030
a great part of, of the strength of, of
the school.

00:39:56.030 --> 00:39:59.500
The final thing I will say and then, then
I'll change the subject here a little.

00:39:59.500 --> 00:40:04.460
Is, is as I look out into the second row I
see I see Bob and Dottie King.

00:40:04.460 --> 00:40:07.477
Will you, will you take a bow.

00:40:07.477 --> 00:40:11.823
So Bob and Dottie as you know about 18
months ago,

00:40:11.823 --> 00:40:15.486
gave an incredible $150 million gift.

00:40:15.486 --> 00:40:18.420
To, to the business school and the
University.

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:24.020
To help us launch a new institute, that's
focused on, on global poverty.

00:40:24.020 --> 00:40:26.490
It's, it's called SEED, the Stanford
Institute for

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:28.460
Innovation in developing economies.

00:40:28.460 --> 00:40:34.157
And I mention it because I have been asked
a, as Dean.

00:40:34.157 --> 00:40:37.469
You know if you, if you talk about global
poverty, the,

00:40:37.469 --> 00:40:41.280
the next words out of your mouth are
usually not business school.

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:42.775
Right?
And, and so,

00:40:42.775 --> 00:40:49.505
what is a business school doing taking on
something not just of, of this magnitude.

00:40:49.505 --> 00:40:53.592
But seemingly this different from from
your heritage.

00:40:53.592 --> 00:40:56.730
And of course for us it's about
entrepreneurship and

00:40:56.730 --> 00:41:01.150
about manage manages of growing
enterprises and developing economies.

00:41:01.150 --> 00:41:04.360
Both of which are things we we know a lot
about.

00:41:04.360 --> 00:41:07.746
But I think our willingness to take that
on goes back to the,

00:41:07.746 --> 00:41:10.342
the roots in the public management
program.

00:41:10.342 --> 00:41:15.668
And the idea that we're educating, not
just functional reasons which will

00:41:15.668 --> 00:41:21.470
take on, you know, specific functions in
corporations, but real leaders.

00:41:21.470 --> 00:41:23.150
Who can take on the big challenges.

00:41:23.150 --> 00:41:26.270
And, and that's one of the challenges that
they're taking on.

00:41:26.270 --> 00:41:30.240
And represents another step, I think in
the evolution of the school.

00:41:31.690 --> 00:41:35.900
So I'd like you to reflect, and I'm not
gonna go in any order, you

00:41:35.900 --> 00:41:39.990
can take this in, in, in any order you'd
like, Audrey, you wanted to jump in first?

00:41:39.990 --> 00:41:41.500
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'd like to comment.

00:41:41.500 --> 00:41:44.650
That one of the criticisms against the
public management program.

00:41:44.650 --> 00:41:48.690
Is so very few people of our graduates
would end up

00:41:48.690 --> 00:41:50.290
public work after they graduated.

00:41:50.290 --> 00:41:50.940
Well they couldn't.

00:41:50.940 --> 00:41:54.860
The salaries were too low after they paid
all the costs here at Stanford.

00:41:54.860 --> 00:41:59.280
But I, I maintain that was very successful
program to broaden their curriculum for

00:41:59.280 --> 00:42:00.149
a number of reasons.

00:42:01.350 --> 00:42:05.900
Yeah.
&gt;&gt; First of all, our students, men or

00:42:05.900 --> 00:42:07.160
women that go into business.

00:42:07.160 --> 00:42:10.870
I think make more successful businessmen
because of

00:42:10.870 --> 00:42:14.280
the broader training that they're had here
at Stanford.

00:42:14.280 --> 00:42:15.620
If something pops up on.

00:42:17.410 --> 00:42:23.050
The need for air and water cleaning and
safety or rising wages.

00:42:23.050 --> 00:42:25.150
They know what the other world is about,
or

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:28.010
they'll respond to it more, more
successfully.

00:42:29.230 --> 00:42:33.280
So, next business is the most powerful
force in our society,

00:42:34.340 --> 00:42:37.860
and business men, well educated.

00:42:37.860 --> 00:42:42.100
Would support needed social legislation
instead of opposing it.

00:42:43.510 --> 00:42:48.590
I believe that 25 years from now, we, this
generation will be severely criticized for

00:42:48.590 --> 00:42:52.360
not doing more in the area of climate
control.

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:58.550
Also, I believe that our graduates with,
with the broader base they get.

00:42:58.550 --> 00:43:01.600
From the dual disciplines and other things
you've been hearing about up here.

00:43:02.950 --> 00:43:07.800
Will be more willing to participate in
community life.

00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:12.070
They'll go on school boards, part time
political appointments.

00:43:12.070 --> 00:43:16.790
And then later on, after they've, kids are
though college and

00:43:16.790 --> 00:43:19.880
they're got secure financial arrangements.

00:43:19.880 --> 00:43:23.320
They may accept full time political
employment they might even run for

00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:26.880
office, and take other, non-profit jobs.

00:43:26.880 --> 00:43:29.022
Some of them might even turn to
philanthropism.

00:43:29.022 --> 00:43:34.230
I mean word here from Bob King and his
wife and Phil Knight and Bob Bass so

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:36.360
that can't be all that bad.

00:43:37.470 --> 00:43:42.720
And then lastly, I think our graduates
will lead a more satisfying life.

00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:49.360
Because they will have, have a broader
viewpoint of what the world is about.

00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:52.970
The fact that they made some contribution
other than in business.

00:43:52.970 --> 00:43:54.610
Will make them feel like they've had a,

00:43:55.860 --> 00:43:58.930
a part in making this world a better place
in which to live.

00:44:01.160 --> 00:44:03.500
A magazine man once said that,

00:44:03.500 --> 00:44:07.240
described Stanford as the business school
with a conscience.

00:44:07.240 --> 00:44:08.010
I like that.

00:44:08.010 --> 00:44:11.250
I think that is an advantage that we can
play on in the years ahead.

00:44:11.250 --> 00:44:12.650
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:44:12.650 --> 00:44:17.935
So maybe we can follow up on that, because
one of the things that, that [UNKNOWN] and

00:44:17.935 --> 00:44:18.690
I spoke about.

00:44:18.690 --> 00:44:20.770
Expansion of the centers while you were
dean.

00:44:20.770 --> 00:44:24.000
And one of the centers that you launched
was the Center for Social Innovation.

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:26.000
So would you, would you talk a little bit
about that,

00:44:26.000 --> 00:44:30.070
and the connection of that to, to this
worker and public management?

00:44:30.070 --> 00:44:31.610
&gt;&gt; Well it really, I think the Center for

00:44:31.610 --> 00:44:36.800
Social Innovation really built on RJ's
vision for public management.

00:44:36.800 --> 00:44:42.470
It was a broader vision to pretty much
echo what he just described.

00:44:42.470 --> 00:44:47.280
That it's not just about going into public
service or managing public enterprises.

00:44:47.280 --> 00:44:48.250
That's important.

00:44:48.250 --> 00:44:53.424
Can we get better management in non-profit
NGO and public enterprises?

00:44:53.424 --> 00:44:58.771
But can we get a different kinda
management in our for-profit enterprises?

00:44:58.771 --> 00:45:02.782
and, and a way of thinking and a way of
approaching problems.

00:45:03.966 --> 00:45:05.458
I was here before R.J.

00:45:05.458 --> 00:45:10.090
when Lee Bok was a great teacher and I was
privileged to have him for class.

00:45:10.090 --> 00:45:13.740
And, he had a class on government and
society and business and

00:45:13.740 --> 00:45:16.760
he'd always end every class with what's
the role of business in this?

00:45:16.760 --> 00:45:20.320
We'd have some big public problem and he
said well what's the role of business?

00:45:20.320 --> 00:45:23.170
And he pushed us hard, we need to do that
more again, we need to,

00:45:23.170 --> 00:45:24.750
you know things come back.

00:45:24.750 --> 00:45:26.560
And I think with our feeling with the
Center for

00:45:26.560 --> 00:45:28.830
Social Innovation was what's the role of
business?

00:45:28.830 --> 00:45:30.490
How can business make this better?

00:45:30.490 --> 00:45:31.790
I mean, business is doing so

00:45:31.790 --> 00:45:35.930
well, enjoying so many advantages from
globalization as Mike says.

00:45:37.130 --> 00:45:41.780
That it has to be feeling a sense of
responsibility for these much,

00:45:41.780 --> 00:45:44.930
much bigger problems that the university
has a capacity to tackle.

00:45:47.170 --> 00:45:51.970
&gt;&gt; So, I wonder if we can give the
audience a little bit of of an insight

00:45:51.970 --> 00:45:57.030
into, of what it's like to sit in the
chairs that each of you have sat in and

00:45:57.030 --> 00:46:01.540
in particular what would you say is the
biggest challenge.

00:46:01.540 --> 00:46:03.110
If you were to pick one.

00:46:03.110 --> 00:46:07.290
The single biggest challenge that you
faced as Dean.

00:46:07.290 --> 00:46:11.000
And if you look back, and I know it's hard
to focus on one thing,

00:46:11.000 --> 00:46:13.710
what would you say was your single
biggest-

00:46:13.710 --> 00:46:14.480
&gt;&gt; Challenge?

00:46:14.480 --> 00:46:16.130
&gt;&gt; Well challenge and then accomplishment.

00:46:18.020 --> 00:46:18.786
Would you take both of those [UNKNOWN].

00:46:18.786 --> 00:46:23.157
&gt;&gt; Well, when I was Dean,.

00:46:23.157 --> 00:46:27.461
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:46:27.461 --> 00:46:33.601
Certainly one of the biggest challenges
was is in the 80s technology

00:46:33.601 --> 00:46:39.324
was just exploding and you know, what,
personal computers

00:46:39.324 --> 00:46:45.170
sort of came out in force in 81 or 82, or
that not very long ago.

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:50.940
And, and you, you can imagine how how

00:46:54.190 --> 00:46:57.440
does that effect the school and how should
we exploit it?

00:46:57.440 --> 00:46:59.850
It was clearly a very powerful force.

00:47:01.650 --> 00:47:02.660
And, but,

00:47:02.660 --> 00:47:07.940
you had to sorta figure out things like
what do you do about the library?

00:47:09.590 --> 00:47:10.610
You keep buying books?

00:47:10.610 --> 00:47:13.380
Or, then, what, what do you do about,
you've got all

00:47:13.380 --> 00:47:17.610
this computing technology out there, but
it didn't talk to one another.

00:47:17.610 --> 00:47:22.686
And so you wanted to embed it in the in
the teaching programs.

00:47:22.686 --> 00:47:27.600
But you can't get faculty due to course
development to do

00:47:27.600 --> 00:47:30.710
that if they don't know what the machinery
is.

00:47:30.710 --> 00:47:35.600
And it couldn't just, you couldn't just
say to students, bring your own computer,

00:47:35.600 --> 00:47:37.660
it wouldn't, how would they communicate?

00:47:39.350 --> 00:47:44.530
And it was almost like that's, that's one
reason I thought in 1990 it was

00:47:44.530 --> 00:47:50.370
a pretty good time to get out, Michael,
because [LAUGH] technology I'm sure

00:47:50.370 --> 00:47:55.470
beca became sort of the single biggest
number in the budget.

00:47:55.470 --> 00:47:57.700
And ours was only, I don't know, ten, 12
million.

00:47:57.700 --> 00:47:59.040
Yours was 180 million, you know.

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:04.300
But, but that was that was part of it.

00:48:04.300 --> 00:48:05.820
How do you deal with that?

00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:11.665
And you know that the [SOUND] the school
to its credit said.

00:48:11.665 --> 00:48:16.196
The only way to do it if we're gonna build
it into the, the teaching programs is

00:48:16.196 --> 00:48:21.020
you can bring whatever computer you want
but there'll be a computer here.

00:48:21.020 --> 00:48:24.130
That the, that the faculty can count on.

00:48:24.130 --> 00:48:27.040
And so we almost created a second library.

00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:30.890
Like we, you know, its a sort of a
computer services.

00:48:32.616 --> 00:48:37.284
The other things was globalization, which
you guys have made,

00:48:37.284 --> 00:48:41.693
you've got more political economy in this
school now than we

00:48:41.693 --> 00:48:46.380
ever dreamed I think it'd have back in the
80's.

00:48:46.380 --> 00:48:50.900
And then in business and changing
environment.

00:48:50.900 --> 00:48:55.230
What it, sort of, changed you had to
understand the political process.

00:48:55.230 --> 00:48:56.969
So go and hire a political scientist.

00:48:58.610 --> 00:49:04.430
I mean we became like, especially under
the 90s and

00:49:04.430 --> 00:49:08.355
the end of the 2000s, you're almost like a
mini university.

00:49:08.355 --> 00:49:10.580
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:49:10.580 --> 00:49:14.340
&gt;&gt; So you, you draw much better on

00:49:14.340 --> 00:49:17.910
the university as well as the silicon
valley community.

00:49:19.110 --> 00:49:22.450
Was there something like 50, 50, 60,
lecturers on this [UNKNOWN].

00:49:22.450 --> 00:49:23.370
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:49:23.370 --> 00:49:25.050
&gt;&gt; Most of them drawn from the community.

00:49:26.180 --> 00:49:29.660
You've got courtesy appointments that run
both ways that

00:49:29.660 --> 00:49:33.430
probably number in the 30's or 40's?

00:49:33.430 --> 00:49:37.840
30, 40, pe, of our people who hold
courtesy appointments in other

00:49:37.840 --> 00:49:40.110
disciplinary departments and the same
coming in.

00:49:40.110 --> 00:49:45.040
You've gotta be a respected member of the
academic community in

00:49:45.040 --> 00:49:46.010
order to make that happen.

00:49:47.070 --> 00:49:49.600
But I think it was either, sorta the
technology and

00:49:49.600 --> 00:49:54.740
the globalization that, that, that were
th,

00:49:54.740 --> 00:50:00.160
those were the biggest issues that I, I at
least they were two very big issues.

00:50:00.160 --> 00:50:05.960
&gt;&gt; And, and if you were to point to a
single accomplishment from

00:50:05.960 --> 00:50:11.820
your dean-ship that your, your proud of.

00:50:11.820 --> 00:50:12.710
&gt;&gt; I you know,
&gt;&gt; Easy.

00:50:12.710 --> 00:50:15.370
&gt;&gt; Deans don't accomplish that much.

00:50:15.370 --> 00:50:16.720
&gt;&gt; That's right.

00:50:16.720 --> 00:50:19.228
Yeah, you're in the right place at the
right time.

00:50:19.228 --> 00:50:23.420
This, this [UNKNOWN] RJ once told me that,

00:50:23.420 --> 00:50:26.660
that, I said, RJ, you're the expert on
this.

00:50:27.800 --> 00:50:33.360
What, what are the characteristics of an
outstanding CEO or leader?

00:50:33.360 --> 00:50:34.390
You remember what you told me?

00:50:35.490 --> 00:50:38.580
Said, the most important thing is, huh?

00:50:38.580 --> 00:50:40.135
&gt;&gt; The dogs have to eat the dog food.

00:50:40.135 --> 00:50:42.790
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; No, that wasn't it.

00:50:42.790 --> 00:50:44.640
You, you told me,

00:50:44.640 --> 00:50:50.520
the most important thing is that the CEO
has to love the organization.

00:50:52.040 --> 00:50:54.600
That all sets a lot of mistakes you know?

00:50:54.600 --> 00:50:59.040
Because and then you could name people
that were

00:50:59.040 --> 00:51:01.760
you thought maybe not all that good in the
technical sense.

00:51:01.760 --> 00:51:03.210
But they love the organization.

00:51:03.210 --> 00:51:04.350
They were just outstanding.

00:51:06.330 --> 00:51:09.000
That's the way I feel about this
institution.

00:51:10.160 --> 00:51:13.838
And it's the way everybody feels about the
institution.

00:51:13.838 --> 00:51:17.614
Think what would happened if we held this
event and there was nobody out there.

00:51:17.614 --> 00:51:18.339
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:51:18.339 --> 00:51:22.236
&gt;&gt; You know, and that hasn't happened, you
know it,

00:51:22.236 --> 00:51:26.640
all these people love the organization as
much as we do.

00:51:26.640 --> 00:51:31.132
That's the, that's, that's the thing I
remember the most.

00:51:31.132 --> 00:51:36.830
&gt;&gt; The main, the main job of the Dean is
to create an environment so

00:51:36.830 --> 00:51:39.610
the professors can go to work
successfully.

00:51:40.750 --> 00:51:42.950
My big challenge was something I failed
in.

00:51:44.480 --> 00:51:49.770
I thought that criteria for admission to
the school was terribly important and

00:51:49.770 --> 00:51:53.150
I tried to get some professor interested
in that subject but

00:51:53.150 --> 00:51:55.310
failed during the ten years.

00:51:55.310 --> 00:51:58.160
You may not remember this Garth but when
you and

00:51:58.160 --> 00:52:03.570
I were having lunch with Jim Van Horne I
mentioned the subject and

00:52:03.570 --> 00:52:08.900
a few days later you came up with two
professors that were interested in it.

00:52:08.900 --> 00:52:12.530
Two great ones [UNKNOWN] and, and Otis.

00:52:12.530 --> 00:52:16.820
And, a few days late Jim Van Horne and I
got with them.

00:52:16.820 --> 00:52:21.720
And they said they were willing along with
Eric Bolton who he'll be the director of

00:52:21.720 --> 00:52:26.740
admissions to undertake a robust
multi-year study

00:52:26.740 --> 00:52:28.818
on criteria for [UNKNOWN].

00:52:28.818 --> 00:52:32.270
With over fifteen students making an
application,

00:52:32.270 --> 00:52:35.930
trying to pick the one that's gonna be a
leader [LAUGH].

00:52:35.930 --> 00:52:39.670
In his later life, a leader is something
more than just making money,

00:52:39.670 --> 00:52:40.720
it's a real challenge.

00:52:41.860 --> 00:52:47.020
They said that they would not only follow
the ones that were successful, but

00:52:47.020 --> 00:52:49.200
also the ones that failed.

00:52:49.200 --> 00:52:50.755
Harvard turned down Warren Buffett.

00:52:50.755 --> 00:52:52.500
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Mm.

00:52:52.500 --> 00:52:58.000
&gt;&gt; I, I'm sure that these gentlemen will
turn out a great study

00:52:58.000 --> 00:53:02.298
that will be extremely useful and very
important, and I waited for the results.

00:53:02.298 --> 00:53:03.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well.

00:53:03.690 --> 00:53:07.820
[LAUGH] And we thank you for, for for your
help with RJ,

00:53:07.820 --> 00:53:10.600
I think it's a incredibly important
subject.

00:53:11.740 --> 00:53:12.700
Mike, your challenges.

00:53:14.300 --> 00:53:17.520
&gt;&gt; Well talked a bit about the my, I mean
the out, let me talk about the experience.

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:22.570
My predecessor at Harvard was a very
funny, talented man named Hen.

00:53:22.570 --> 00:53:26.930
It's very skilled academic politician
named Henry Rosovsky.

00:53:26.930 --> 00:53:28.980
And he was once asked what's it like being
dean?

00:53:28.980 --> 00:53:31.190
And he said it's like being a dentist.

00:53:31.190 --> 00:53:33.725
You see somebody every half hour and
inflict pain.

00:53:33.725 --> 00:53:35.875
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:53:35.875 --> 00:53:39.770
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:53:39.770 --> 00:53:42.550
you know, not every aspect of a job is
fun.

00:53:42.550 --> 00:53:48.220
I mean, overall it's fun but it's you know
you get to see a lot of people.

00:53:48.220 --> 00:53:50.215
Some of them have fairly peculiar
requests.

00:53:50.215 --> 00:53:54.050
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'll tell you the, it,

00:53:54.050 --> 00:53:58.150
the episode that, that I felt and still do
feel the most badly.

00:53:58.150 --> 00:54:02.260
I mean, when your a Dean, you're
ultimately responsible for a,

00:54:02.260 --> 00:54:05.960
a pretty wide range of functions, as you
are in business.

00:54:05.960 --> 00:54:12.480
And this was the early stages of fairly
as, as Bob Jedeki just described, fairly

00:54:12.480 --> 00:54:17.410
rapid expansion of computer systems, and
you know, we weren't that good at it.

00:54:17.410 --> 00:54:20.139
And at one point, a back up failed badly.

00:54:20.139 --> 00:54:21.380
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.

00:54:21.380 --> 00:54:24.770
&gt;&gt; And we lost some PhD thesis or parts of
them and

00:54:24.770 --> 00:54:30.350
what not, and we got a fair amount of it
back but, but if I had to pick one

00:54:31.490 --> 00:54:36.165
moment if you like, in which I thought
gosh, I'd really rather have another job.

00:54:36.165 --> 00:54:36.840
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:54:36.840 --> 00:54:39.710
&gt;&gt; That, that was, that was, that was
probably it.

00:54:41.120 --> 00:54:43.870
The most rewarding part I think has
already been described.

00:54:43.870 --> 00:54:48.720
It's working with with staff and people.

00:54:48.720 --> 00:54:51.200
It's working with people you really
respect.

00:54:51.200 --> 00:54:55.730
On the faculty, you know, the lecturers
who came in and joined us.

00:54:55.730 --> 00:54:57.520
People of enormous accomplishment and

00:54:57.520 --> 00:55:00.729
commitment in, in RJ's terms, broad
committment.

00:55:01.970 --> 00:55:05.090
To the society and to doing some good.

00:55:05.090 --> 00:55:06.790
So that, that's the most rewarding part.

00:55:06.790 --> 00:55:08.680
And I'm like, Bob Jatki, I don't have a
clue.

00:55:10.190 --> 00:55:14.220
I think it's sort of somebody elses job to
figure if anything useful kind of

00:55:14.220 --> 00:55:15.390
got done on the way through.

00:55:17.140 --> 00:55:18.080
&gt;&gt; All right Bob, I'm learning.

00:55:18.080 --> 00:55:19.755
Let's focus just on the challenges.

00:55:19.755 --> 00:55:22.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:55:22.460 --> 00:55:28.660
&gt;&gt; You know, for me, I, I think by far the
biggest challenge is this balancing

00:55:28.660 --> 00:55:33.610
act among the various constituencies that
make up an academic institution.

00:55:33.610 --> 00:55:36.840
It's really much more challenging than it
is in business.

00:55:36.840 --> 00:55:40.810
You know, you've got students, you've got
faculty, you've got staff,

00:55:40.810 --> 00:55:44.670
you've got alumni, you've got recruiters,
you have the university.

00:55:44.670 --> 00:55:49.290
And it's not gonna be a great school
unless all of those constituents feel they

00:55:49.290 --> 00:55:52.260
have a part in that, and they understand
each other's role, and

00:55:52.260 --> 00:55:52.996
they feel good about it.

00:55:52.996 --> 00:55:58.220
So I, I think it's much more complicated
than it is business, and getting that

00:55:58.220 --> 00:56:01.670
balancing act right and bringing everybody
along and everybody excited, and

00:56:01.670 --> 00:56:05.920
respecting each other's role, for me was
the biggest challenge.

00:56:05.920 --> 00:56:06.544
&gt;&gt; Mm.

00:56:06.544 --> 00:56:12.090
&gt;&gt; The thing I feel the best about, I feel
great about the campus.

00:56:12.090 --> 00:56:12.910
I'm proud of the campus, but

00:56:12.910 --> 00:56:16.700
I feel the best about the relationship
between our school and the university.

00:56:16.700 --> 00:56:19.920
For me, that you know when I, my search
committee, and

00:56:19.920 --> 00:56:21.950
I see one member out there, Jim.

00:56:21.950 --> 00:56:24.550
Said, you know Bob, just keep the
university off our back.

00:56:24.550 --> 00:56:26.960
There was, I mean I'm probably overstating
that.

00:56:26.960 --> 00:56:32.200
But, you know, I, look I grew up in a life
and where I was a group executive, and

00:56:32.200 --> 00:56:35.910
you wanted to keep headquarters away, and
you wanted to keep the CEO

00:56:35.910 --> 00:56:38.685
kind of not sure what was going on in your
line of business.

00:56:38.685 --> 00:56:40.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:56:40.400 --> 00:56:42.690
&gt;&gt; But I also have been a CEO for six
years and

00:56:42.690 --> 00:56:45.510
I wonder, why aren't these group
executives working better together and

00:56:45.510 --> 00:56:48.220
how can we get them to think about the
whole, and make the whole better?

00:56:49.680 --> 00:56:54.680
And I just felt that we have a tremendous
role to play with the university and

00:56:54.680 --> 00:56:57.640
vice versa, to make it a great university.

00:56:57.640 --> 00:57:00.490
And I feel extremely good today about how
respected our school

00:57:01.880 --> 00:57:06.360
is within Stanford and the role that our
school both students,

00:57:06.360 --> 00:57:10.660
faculty everybody is playing to make
Stanford a better university.

00:57:10.660 --> 00:57:13.950
To me I am the most proud of that it
wasn't something that I did but

00:57:13.950 --> 00:57:17.490
it was a cultural change that's in some
ways much harder to

00:57:17.490 --> 00:57:19.920
bring about than a physical structure like
building a campus.

00:57:19.920 --> 00:57:22.610
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:57:22.610 --> 00:57:24.510
You know we're, we're almost coming up on
time,

00:57:24.510 --> 00:57:28.630
but I have one more question which I'm
gonna, I'm gonna pose to Mike Spence,

00:57:28.630 --> 00:57:33.470
because I think history will not be kind
to us in retrospect,

00:57:33.470 --> 00:57:38.350
if if we close without a forward looking
question, of the following kind.

00:57:38.350 --> 00:57:40.990
And the question, Mike, is this.

00:57:40.990 --> 00:57:42.600
So in 1999, Mike stepped down.

00:57:43.620 --> 00:57:45.810
And and I thought well, here's a real
opportunity.

00:57:45.810 --> 00:57:50.090
I got a hold of Mike and I said now that
you no longer have a day job

00:57:51.300 --> 00:57:54.130
why don't you join me in teaching a
course?

00:57:54.130 --> 00:57:57.390
Let's teach a course on electronic
business.

00:57:57.390 --> 00:57:59.590
You know it was the height of the dot com
era.

00:57:59.590 --> 00:58:02.590
I said we have no idea what it is.

00:58:02.590 --> 00:58:05.098
Maybe we'll learn something by teaching a
course.

00:58:05.098 --> 00:58:06.186
And.

00:58:06.186 --> 00:58:07.430
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:58:07.430 --> 00:58:09.140
&gt;&gt; And we did.

00:58:09.140 --> 00:58:09.690
&gt;&gt; Always.

00:58:09.690 --> 00:58:10.210
From the skills.

00:58:10.210 --> 00:58:11.360
&gt;&gt; We, we learned quite a bit.

00:58:11.360 --> 00:58:13.780
And if I were to caricature the course.

00:58:13.780 --> 00:58:16.280
I don't know if you agree, but if you were
to caricature the course,

00:58:16.280 --> 00:58:17.890
the way the course went is,

00:58:17.890 --> 00:58:23.560
every week we brought in some business
leaders from one industry after the other.

00:58:24.650 --> 00:58:29.310
And what they said to us was, they said,
this Internet thing is really interesting.

00:58:30.650 --> 00:58:32.235
But it's not gonna affect our industry.

00:58:32.235 --> 00:58:34.270
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:58:34.270 --> 00:58:37.020
&gt;&gt; You know, books, people are still gonna
have to go to the book store.

00:58:37.020 --> 00:58:39.570
They're gonna have to pick up the book,
they're gonna have to feel it.

00:58:39.570 --> 00:58:40.130
Books are fine.

00:58:41.210 --> 00:58:43.000
Well, book stores are dead.

00:58:43.000 --> 00:58:45.010
You know, travel agencies.

00:58:45.010 --> 00:58:48.090
Well, you're gonna have to go to the
travel, you know.

00:58:48.090 --> 00:58:52.040
The way auto, automobiles are newspaper
content, one after the other.

00:58:52.040 --> 00:58:54.190
And that was basically what the course
consisted of, right?

00:58:54.190 --> 00:58:55.620
As we went through.

00:58:55.620 --> 00:59:01.260
And and conspicuously absent from that
list was higher education.

00:59:01.260 --> 00:59:02.050
&gt;&gt; Mm.

00:59:02.050 --> 00:59:04.760
&gt;&gt; Right?
And yet we look out there today, and

00:59:04.760 --> 00:59:09.470
we look at this revolution that is going
on in, in online education.

00:59:09.470 --> 00:59:14.290
And Coursera, which is one of the the
companies that have spun out of

00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:19.870
Stanford is, is faster to two million
users, than either Facebook or Twitter.

00:59:21.130 --> 00:59:22.760
So this is a revolution.

00:59:22.760 --> 00:59:26.966
I mean, this is no longer a shot across,
across the bow.

00:59:26.966 --> 00:59:31.748
Do you have views on, on, what do you
think this holds for

00:59:31.748 --> 00:59:37.840
higher education, and for, for business
schools in particular?

00:59:37.840 --> 00:59:40.510
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean I think these are major,
major changes I mean,

00:59:40.510 --> 00:59:44.840
first of all in higher education in
America we're gonna have to sort of

00:59:44.840 --> 00:59:49.180
get back in line, much like the health
care sector in terms of cost.

00:59:49.180 --> 00:59:54.910
And, and I think one of the ways were
gonna do that is use

00:59:54.910 --> 01:00:00.480
the digital media that are continually
being reinvented as tools.

01:00:01.580 --> 01:00:04.470
I think the hard part, the thing that
where we are gonna have to

01:00:04.470 --> 01:00:09.340
either experiment or wait through and or
use cutting edge

01:00:09.340 --> 01:00:12.860
research from social scientists you, you
know, psychologists and

01:00:12.860 --> 01:00:18.390
sociologists who study these things, is to
figure out what's, what's the right mix.

01:00:18.390 --> 01:00:21.030
I mean, we know the interpersonal part
isn't gonna go away.

01:00:22.680 --> 01:00:25.800
So we have to, so it's not a clean model.

01:00:25.800 --> 01:00:28.360
It's not the kind of silly stuff we heard
in the chorus.

01:00:28.360 --> 01:00:29.972
You know, one group comes in and

01:00:29.972 --> 01:00:32.901
says online retail will blow away the
bricks and mortar and

01:00:32.901 --> 01:00:37.630
the other people okay, mm, this is a, no,
[LAUGH] they're, they're gonna go.

01:00:37.630 --> 01:00:39.330
And neither one of them is true.

01:00:40.480 --> 01:00:43.310
You know there's a lot of problems in the
world where you

01:00:43.310 --> 01:00:45.690
don't know the answer in advance.

01:00:45.690 --> 01:00:48.880
Even though journalists want you to tell
'em what the answer you, and

01:00:48.880 --> 01:00:55.420
you have to have a, a, a school like that
that has the capacity to,

01:00:55.420 --> 01:01:00.130
and the will and the incentive to adapt
and learn how to do it.

01:01:00.130 --> 01:01:04.340
So, I think we can be, I think we can be a
leader in that but,

01:01:04.340 --> 01:01:10.720
I don't have any doubt whatsoever that,
that in multiple dimensions,

01:01:10.720 --> 01:01:14.120
this will be an important part of the way
that we teach and

01:01:14.120 --> 01:01:16.600
do resarch, well it already is with
research, right?

01:01:16.600 --> 01:01:20.340
I mean you know how, I mean you probably
all know this but

01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:25.790
you mean the Bob Zelleck put the World
Bank database that's just massive,

01:01:25.790 --> 01:01:28.760
you know, it's a public resource now.

01:01:28.760 --> 01:01:29.950
You can go look up anything you want,

01:01:31.080 --> 01:01:35.160
essentially, about any country in the
world or any collection of countries.

